research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-dini-needs-a-compact-domain.result.json:16:  "tail": "VERDICT: CLEAN\n\nBOUNDARY: The counterexample functions are well‑defined and continuous on the closed unbounded interval \\([0,\\infty)\\), including at the left endpoint \\(x=0\\); the pointwise limit is \\(0\\) everywhere. The witness \\(x_k = a_k = \\iota(k+1) > 0\\) lies in the domain and yields \\(f_k(x_k) = 1/2\\), which prevents uniform convergence. The domain is closed but not bounded above; Heine‑Borel (L3) correctly classifies it as non‑compact. No degenerate, empty, or isolated‑point cases affect the reasoning.\n\nSOURCES: All nine declared dependencies were supplied in full and inspected: thm‑algebra‑of‑continuous‑functions, def‑continuity‑real, cor‑archimedean‑reciprocal, def‑canonical‑natural, lem‑of‑naturals‑positive, lem‑of‑inverse‑positive, thm‑heine‑borel‑characterisation‑r, def‑bounded‑set, thm‑dini‑on‑a‑closed‑interval. Each supports the target’s usage without mismatch; no hidden hypotheses are required beyond what is cited.\n\nCHECKED: Title, public claim (“the compact‑domain hypothesis in Dini’s theorem can be dropped”), every numbered step (1.1–1.5, 2.1), the surrounding Facts & Assumptions, and the contract’s derivations and boundaries were verified."
